{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-167640",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{13\\pi }{4}$ that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{13\\pi }{4}-2\\pi  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{13\\pi }{4})=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}).$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14264596/assets/e169b457c3216ffb18463b3e37a345b708e2d5efb2af1076b25f397867d7a826.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})}=\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}=-\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+7=7-\\sqrt{2}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
